WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:04.437
Lillian Bonsignore:
This is the cool part.

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:05.805
This is the cool
part of the job.

00:00:05.805 --> 00:00:07.140
Lots of lives are
gonna be saved

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.342
by these people,
lots of lives.

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:11.411
Narrator: This is Lillian
Bonsignore,

00:00:11.411 --> 00:00:12.812
The Fire Department
of New York's

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:16.416
very first female and
openly gay EMS chief

00:00:16.416 --> 00:00:20.086
in the organization's
154-year history.

00:00:20.086 --> 00:00:22.222
Bonsignore: I talk about
my wife openly.

00:00:22.222 --> 00:00:25.291
So it's OK that we
have this conversation.

00:00:25.291 --> 00:00:26.326
I'm comfortable with it.

00:00:26.326 --> 00:00:28.428
I think there are lots of folks

00:00:28.428 --> 00:00:30.997
that need examples and
role models,

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:32.632
that need to know that,

00:00:32.632 --> 00:00:35.068
whatever box somebody is
willing to put you in,

00:00:35.068 --> 00:00:37.303
doesn't mean you
have to live there.

00:00:37.303 --> 00:00:38.304
Narrator: Lillian started out

00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:40.240
as an emergency
medical technician

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.143
on an ambulance in
the Bronx 28 years ago,

00:00:43.143 --> 00:00:44.778
and, since, she's worked
her way up

00:00:44.778 --> 00:00:46.579
to the highest-ranking
position.

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:48.248
She now runs the largest

00:00:48.248 --> 00:00:50.050
emergency-medical-system
force

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.085
in the entire United States.

00:00:52.085 --> 00:00:53.987
Her team's a part of
the fire department,

00:00:53.987 --> 00:00:56.489
but these folks are
paramedics and EMTs.

00:00:56.489 --> 00:00:58.058
They work on ambulances,

00:00:58.058 --> 00:00:59.926
respond to 911 calls,

00:00:59.926 --> 00:01:02.062
and provide on-the-scene
medical care.

00:01:02.062 --> 00:01:04.230
Bonsignore: That means
there are 4,000-plus

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.133
EMTs and paramedics that
staff our ambulances,

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:08.935
that service
our five boroughs,

00:01:08.935 --> 00:01:10.603
and my job is to
oversee that

00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:12.605
and make sure that
we are providing

00:01:12.605 --> 00:01:14.841
the highest level
of medical care

00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:16.910
to those people who
need us the most.

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:17.811
Man: Everybody clear?

00:01:17.811 --> 00:01:19.045
Clear, Joey?

00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:20.680
Everybody clear?

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.382
Continue CPR.

00:01:22.382 --> 00:01:23.683
Narrator: Born and
raised in the Bronx,

00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:26.052
Lillian says she had
a tough childhood

00:01:26.052 --> 00:01:29.289
and credits her success to
a few key women in her life.

00:01:29.289 --> 00:01:31.691
Bonsignore: Along my
way, very strong women

00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:34.861
just magically found
themselves in my path,

00:01:34.861 --> 00:01:36.896
and they decided
to take an interest

00:01:36.896 --> 00:01:39.265
in a kid that needed
help and, you know,

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:40.900
maybe was a little
broken growing up

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:44.304
in a tough neighborhood
with difficult circumstances.

00:01:44.304 --> 00:01:46.506
Narrator: It was actually
Lillian's pediatrician

00:01:46.506 --> 00:01:48.808
who suggested she
become an EMT.

00:01:48.808 --> 00:01:50.910
Bonsignore: I didn't know
what an EMT was,

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.446
but I trusted her with
everything that I had,

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:55.648
and I took this job
for the summer,

00:01:55.648 --> 00:01:57.984
and it's been 28 years,

00:01:57.984 --> 00:02:00.453
and it's the longest
summer job I've ever had.

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:01.554
Narrator: She moved up the
ranks

00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:02.989
in the Bronx and Brooklyn

00:02:02.989 --> 00:02:05.492
to lieutenant, captain,
and station commander.

00:02:05.492 --> 00:02:06.126
Along the way,

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:08.661
she became an instructor
at the EMS Academy

00:02:08.661 --> 00:02:11.498
and endured the grueling
paramedic's training.

00:02:11.498 --> 00:02:13.500
She was also among
the first responders

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.502
during the terrorist
attacks on 9/11.

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:18.872
When Lillian was 30 and
serving on the force,

00:02:18.872 --> 00:02:20.173
she came out as gay.

00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:22.175
She's now married and
has two daughters.

00:02:22.175 --> 00:02:24.277
Bonsignore: There have
been a couple of men who

00:02:24.277 --> 00:02:26.279
maybe believed that
a woman's place

00:02:26.279 --> 00:02:28.548
is not in the
fire department,

00:02:28.548 --> 00:02:30.650
and, you know, they
don't really appreciate

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:33.019
maybe an alternative
home situation,

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:36.122
but that is their
issue to come around to,

00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:38.191
that's not really
my concern,

00:02:38.191 --> 00:02:40.527
but I will save their
lives as quickly

00:02:40.527 --> 00:02:41.728
as I would save the life

00:02:41.728 --> 00:02:42.862
of the woman
that I've loved

00:02:42.862 --> 00:02:43.997
for 18 years.

00:02:43.997 --> 00:02:45.431
Narrator: After 28 years
of service,

00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:48.101
in May 2019,
Lillian was promoted

00:02:48.101 --> 00:02:50.436
to the fire department's
chief of EMS,

00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:52.639
serving the entirety
of New York City.

00:02:52.639 --> 00:02:54.073
Bonsignore: Today, I can
stand here

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:55.041
and accept a promotion

00:02:55.041 --> 00:02:58.211
to the highest position
in FDNY EMS,

00:02:58.211 --> 00:03:00.313
even though I'm a woman.

00:03:01.147 --> 00:03:05.084
Today, I can stand here next
to the woman that I love,

00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:08.288
and enjoy the spirit
of this special day,

00:03:08.288 --> 00:03:10.423
when there are more
places in this country

00:03:10.423 --> 00:03:12.592
that you could be fired
for being a member

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:14.561
of the LGBTQ community.

00:03:14.561 --> 00:03:16.296
Those females that
are out there and

00:03:16.296 --> 00:03:17.530
didn't have a role model,

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.332
and didn't have
somebody to say that,

00:03:19.332 --> 00:03:21.234
yes, this is a possibility
for you,

00:03:21.234 --> 00:03:22.101
now they do,

00:03:22.101 --> 00:03:23.836
and I take that
responsibility

00:03:23.836 --> 00:03:24.804
on wholeheartedly.

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:26.539
Narrator: But Lillian isn't

00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:28.841
entering the role at
the easiest of times.

00:03:28.841 --> 00:03:32.278
Call volume is up,
having risen 4.6%

00:03:32.278 --> 00:03:34.581
from 2017 to 2018.

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:37.050
EMS responded to
a record-breaking

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.819
1.5 million
911 calls last year.

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.256
EMTs are making the move
to higher-paid positions,

00:03:43.256 --> 00:03:45.491
like firefighting,
at a staggering rate,

00:03:46.359 --> 00:03:48.962
and response times across
EMS in New York City

00:03:48.962 --> 00:03:51.431
have risen to
life-threatening heights.

00:03:51.431 --> 00:03:52.732
Bonsignore: This is a
very tough job.

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:54.000
That is something that

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.435
people don't
really recognize.

00:03:55.435 --> 00:03:57.403
Sometimes they
think of EMS

00:03:57.403 --> 00:03:59.239
as being
ambulance drivers,

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:00.807
but the reality is,

00:04:00.807 --> 00:04:04.377
the things that EMS
people see in their jobs,

00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:05.979
let's say over the
course of a month,

00:04:05.979 --> 00:04:07.947
may be things that
people will never see

00:04:07.947 --> 00:04:09.649
in the course of
their lifetime.

00:04:09.649 --> 00:04:11.951
Narrator: Lillian says
her EMTs and paramedics

00:04:11.951 --> 00:04:14.721
are also facing a growing
array of daily issues:

00:04:14.721 --> 00:04:16.889
long hours,
assault from patients,

00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:18.958
patients with
infectious diseases,

00:04:18.958 --> 00:04:21.694
and, sometimes, dangerous
service locations.

00:04:21.694 --> 00:04:23.129
She's on a mission
to make the public

00:04:23.129 --> 00:04:24.831
aware of these realities

00:04:24.831 --> 00:04:26.866
and honor those
who take on the sacrifice.

00:04:26.866 --> 00:04:28.868
Bonsignore: These people
come every single day

00:04:28.868 --> 00:04:30.470
with a smile on their face,

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:32.705
knowing that
these challenges exist,

00:04:32.705 --> 00:04:35.608
and they work to
push past these things

00:04:35.608 --> 00:04:38.211
just because they
know it's right.

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:40.146
Narrator: As for Lillian,
she loves the job,

00:04:40.146 --> 00:04:41.681
and plans to use
her new position

00:04:41.681 --> 00:04:43.483
to lift up young
folks in her community

00:04:43.483 --> 00:04:45.318
struggling like
she once was.

00:04:45.318 --> 00:04:48.187
Bonsignore: We have a
very high suicide rate

00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:50.690
in the young LGBTQ
community,

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:53.092
and they need us.

00:04:53.092 --> 00:04:55.395
They need somebody
to invest in them

00:04:55.395 --> 00:04:57.730
like these women
invested in me.

00:04:57.730 --> 00:04:59.365
It was truly life-changing,

00:04:59.365 --> 00:05:02.235
and if you think a few
minutes doesn't matter

00:05:02.235 --> 00:05:03.703
in somebody else's life,

00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:05.305
I can tell you
that you're wrong.

00:05:05.305 --> 00:05:06.572
If you think that,

00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:08.274
if you invest heavily

00:05:08.274 --> 00:05:09.842
 in somebody
who's struggling,

00:05:09.842 --> 00:05:11.344
that they won't
appreciate it

00:05:11.344 --> 00:05:13.680
or it won't guide
them to success,

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:15.481
I can tell you that
you're wrong

00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:17.517
because the only reason
that I'm here today

00:05:17.517 --> 00:05:18.818
is because that happened.

